// A BARE `{ … }` BLOCK. `vl check` returns 0 and the emitter refuses with `emitProgram:
// unsupported statement in body` — clause 2. The parser mints a real `Block` node for it on
// purpose ("A `{` at statement position is a BLOCK, UNLESS it shapes up as an object
// literal", `parser.vl`), and the checker gives it its own scope: a `const n` inside the
// block does not collide with a `const n` after it, and gets its own unused-variable
// warning. Only the emitter has no arm — `emitStmt` handles a block only where some other
// node OWNS one (an `if` arm, a loop body), never as a statement in its own right.
// Every scope and every content refuses: function body, lambda body, module scope, nested,
// inside an `if`/`while`/`for`. Removing the braces runs.
// See D1253.
// Should print 7.
function go() {
  {
    print(7)
  }
}

go()
